  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather continues through Tuesday with cool \ntemperatures giving way to a warming trend by Tuesday afternoon.\n\nSurface high pressure will briefly build over the eastern Great \nLakes and New England tonight. Diurnal cumulus will dissipate this \nevening with the loss of daytime heating, setting up a good \nradiational cooling night with mainly clear skies and light winds. \nLows will drop back into the 40s areawide with the exception of the \nimmediate lakeshores. Some of the normally colder Southern Tier \nvalleys and Tug Hill region may make a run at the upper 30s. Lows in \nLewis County will likely stay just mild enough to prevent any patchy \nfrost, with a better frost potential found farther east across \nportions of the Adirondacks.\n\nThe high will drift off the New England coast Tuesday, with strong \nwarm advection developing in its wake. This will boost highs well \ninto the 70s in most areas after a cool start to the day. Strong \nwarm advection and moisture transport aloft will bring a northwest \nto southeast increase in high clouds, but dry weather will continue \nto prevail through early evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Several rounds of showers and isolated thunderstorms \nTuesday night through Thursday as a frontal boundary stalls nearby. \n\nThe pattern will turn unsettled again Tuesday night through Thursday \nas a frontal zone oscillates north and south across the Ohio Valley \nand eastern Great Lakes. The most widespread rain will likely be \nWednesday night through Thursday when forcing and moisture transport \nmaximizes.\n\nTuesday night through Wednesday, a weak cold front will move \nsoutheast across the eastern Great Lakes. Most of the large scale \nforcing for ascent will pass well north of the area across Quebec, \nleaving minimal height falls along the weak frontal zone in the \neastern Great Lakes. Weak low level moisture convergence may support \na few scattered showers and possibly an isolated thunderstorm, along \nwith plenty of rain free time as well. \n\nThe frontal zone may briefly sag south of the area into Ohio and PA \nlate Wednesday, then move back north as a weak wave of low pressure \ndevelops and runs east down the boundary. The frontal wave and \ntightening thermal packing along the boundary will bring a period of \nstronger frontogenesis, moisture convergence, and deformation late \nWednesday night through Thursday, with rain becoming more \nwidespread. Enough instability may develop for a few embedded \nthunderstorms as well. The orientation of the frontal zone laying \nout in an east-west fashion parallel to the mid/upper level flow \ncombined with PWAT values reaching or exceeding 1.75\" suggests some \npotential for locally heavy rainfall.\n\nA mid/upper level trough is forecast to sharpen across Quebec \nThursday night, forcing the frontal zone southward with rain ending \nfrom north to south. High pressure will then build just north of the \nGreat Lakes Friday through Saturday, bringing a return to dry \nweather.",
